## Claim
Text: "Generally the deaths from Covid-19 are still pretty flat because we've flattened the curve."
Claim author: Ron Johnson
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did the United States experience a significant increase in COVID-19 deaths during the fall of 2020, after October 21, 2020?
Answer: Yes, the search result indicates that COVID-19 cases accelerated significantly in the United States during the winter following October 21, 2020. It states that "Over the winter, the number of cases accelerated in almost every state" and that "The number of people diagnosed with COVID-19 each day peaked in January of 2021," which implies a substantial surge in cases—and by extension deaths—during the fall and winter of 2020-2021, contradicting the claim that deaths remained "pretty flat."

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/in-depth/graphics/2020/03/10/us-coronavirus-map-tracking-united-states-outbreak/4945223002/

### How many total COVID-19 deaths had been reported in the United States as of October 21, 2020?
Answer: According to the source, the total number of confirmed and probable COVID-19 deaths in the U.S. for the entire year of 2020 was estimated at 377,883. However, the source does not provide the specific cumulative death count as of October 21, 2020 (the date of the claim), only the full-year 2020 total.

Source URL: https://injuryfacts.nsc.org/home-and-community/safety-topics/covid-19-cases-in-the-united-states/

### What is the epidemiological definition of "flattening the curve" and was this definition met in the United States by October 2020?
Answer: According to the source, "flattening the curve" is an epidemiological concept defined by Dr. Drew Harris as spreading out the number of new cases over a longer period of time so that the healthcare system is not overwhelmed, rather than reducing the total number of infections. As Dr. Anthony Fauci explained in the same article, "If you look at the curves of outbreaks, you know, they go up big peaks, and then they come down. What we need to do is flatten that down. That would have less people infected. That would, ultimately, have less deaths." However, the search result does not contain information about whether the United States had met this definition by October 2020, so I cannot answer that part of the question based on this source alone.

