## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Insights

The fact-check reveals that COVID-19 cases and deaths surged significantly in the United States during the fall and winter of 2020-2021, peaking in January 2021. This directly contradicts Senator Johnson's claim that deaths were "pretty flat" as of October 21, 2020. Additionally, the epidemiological concept of "flattening the curve" refers to spreading cases over time to avoid overwhelming healthcare systems—not to keeping deaths flat or low. The claim misapplies this concept as justification for the assertion about flat deaths.

## Information Still Lacking

We lack the specific daily/weekly COVID-19 death trend data for the exact period around October 21, 2020, to determine precisely how "flat" deaths were at that specific moment. However, the fall surge was already underway by late October 2020, and the dramatic winter surge that followed provides strong evidence that the claim's characterization of deaths as "pretty flat" was inaccurate.

## Conclusion

The evidence clearly refutes the claim. COVID-19 deaths were not "pretty flat" around October 21, 2020—the fall surge was already beginning, and deaths surged dramatically in the following weeks and months, peaking in January 2021. Furthermore, the reasoning that deaths were flat "because we've flattened the curve" misapplies the epidemiological concept, which refers to spreading cases over time rather than keeping deaths flat. The claim is therefore `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
COVID-19 cases and deaths surged significantly in the United States during the fall and winter of 2020-2021, with daily cases peaking in January 2021 ([USA Today](https://www.usatoday.com/in-depth/graphics/2020/03/10/us-coronavirus-map-tracking-united-states-outbreak/4945223002/)), directly contradicting the claim that deaths were "pretty flat" as of late October 2020. By the end of 2020, the U.S. had recorded approximately 377,883 COVID-19 deaths ([NSC Injury Facts](https://injuryfacts.nsc.org/home-and-community/safety-topics/covid-19-cases-in-the-united-states/)), with the fall surge already underway at the time of the statement. Furthermore, the claim misapplies the epidemiological concept of "flattening the curve," which—as explained by Dr. Anthony Fauci—refers to spreading cases over time to avoid overwhelming healthcare systems, not to keeping death numbers flat ([NPR](https://www.npr.org/2020/03/11/814603316/public-health-experts-encourage-social-distancing-to-flatten-the-curve-of-infect)). The claim is **refuted**.